#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Measure the ink in every glyph in lib/icons.sh.

A font size is not an icon size. Rendered at the same nominal size, the ink
inside these glyphs varies by more than a factor of two -- the keyboard is 0.43
of an em tall, the battery 0.88 -- so a row set at one size looks arbitrary,
because your eye measures the mark on the screen and not the em box around it.

Worse, three items change their glyph at runtime. The audio device switches
between seven, the battery between twelve, the volume between five. A single
`icon.font` on those items is right for whichever glyph happened to be showing
when it was chosen and wrong for the rest: the built-in speaker was drawn at
the size measured from the AirPods glyph and overflowed its chip by 28%.

WHAT THIS EMITS, AND WHY IT IS NOT A POINT SIZE. Each glyph is rendered very
large and its ink bounding box measured, giving width, height, and how far the
ink's centre sits from the pen the glyph was drawn with -- all as fractions of
the em. Those three numbers are facts about the font. The point size derived
from them is not: it depends on how tall the chip is, which is a skin's
decision. So this writes the fractions and lib/icons.sh does the arithmetic at
load, from the three numbers the current skin supplies:

    ICON_TARGET   the optical size every glyph is normalised to
    ICON_MAX_W    ink may not be wider than this
    ICON_MAX_H    ...nor taller, or it escapes the chip

Keeping the caps with the chip height is the point. They were constants here,
measured against a 29-point chip, and a skin with a 20-point chip inherited
them silently -- a cap that no longer binds is a glyph that overflows. Now a
chip height cannot be chosen without the caps that go with it.

    ./bin/measure-icons > lib/ink.sh

WHY THE INK'S CENTRE. sketchybar will not centre a glyph for you: the width it
measures for one is the ink's right edge rounded up, with the left side bearing
thrown away, so the box is wider than the glyph on the right side only and the
glyph sits left of the middle. lib/icons.sh places the pen itself instead, and
the only number that lets it is where the ink's centre actually is.

WHY sqrt(w*h). The size that makes two different glyphs look equally big is
the one that equalises the *optical* size -- not the height, which would leave
the flat wide keyboard half again as wide as everything beside it, and not the
width, which barely varies at all in a monospaced font.

This needs Pillow and is a development tool. Nothing at runtime depends on it:
its output is committed, so the bar starts with no Python.
"""

import os
import re
import sys

from PIL import Image, ImageDraw, ImageFont

FONT = os.path.expanduser("~/Library/Fonts/HackNerdFontMono-Bold.ttf")
BASE = 200          # render size; large enough that ink is exact to a pixel

here = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
src = open(os.path.join(here, "lib", "icons.sh"), encoding="utf-8").read()
icons = re.findall(r'^export (ICON_\w+)="([^"]*)"', src, re.M)

font = ImageFont.truetype(FONT, BASE)


def ink(ch):
    im = Image.new("L", (BASE * 3, BASE * 3), 0)
    ImageDraw.Draw(im).text((BASE, BASE), ch, font=font, fill=255)
    box = im.getbbox()
    if not box:
        return None
    # The pen was put down at x = BASE, so the third number is where the ink's
    # centre falls relative to the pen -- which is the only thing that lets a
    # glyph be placed by hand. See the note above on why it is needed.
    return ((box[2] - box[0]) / BASE,
            (box[3] - box[1]) / BASE,
            ((box[0] + box[2]) / 2 - BASE) / BASE)


print("# Generated by bin/measure-icons -- do not hand-edit.")
print("# The ink in each glyph, as a fraction of the em: name, width, height,")
print("# and how far the ink's centre sits from the pen it was drawn with.")
print("# A fact about the font, so no point size appears here -- lib/icons.sh")
print("# turns these into sizes using the chip height the current skin chose.")
print("# Regenerate after adding or changing any glyph in lib/icons.sh.")
print("ICON_INK='\\")
missing = []
for name, ch in icons:
    if not ch:
        continue
    m = ink(ch)
    if not m:
        missing.append(name)
        continue
    w, h, cx = m
    print('%s %.4f %.4f %.4f' % (name[len("ICON_"):], w, h, cx))
print("'")

if missing:
    sys.stderr.write("no ink for: %s\n" % ", ".join(missing))
    sys.exit(1)
